Working of the Full System (AI-Based Elephant Intrusion Detection System)
Step 1: Continuous Forest Monitoring

Each monitoring unit is installed along forest boundaries or near villages. The unit consists of:

Jetson Nano (AI Processing Unit)
RGB Camera
PIR Motion Sensor
Buzzer
LoRaWAN Module
Solar Panel with Battery Backup

The system continuously monitors the surrounding area for any movement.

Step 2: Motion Detection

The PIR sensor continuously detects infrared radiation emitted by moving objects.

If no movement is detected, the system remains in low-power mode.
When motion is detected, the camera and AI module are activated.

This reduces unnecessary power consumption and increases the system's efficiency.

Step 3: Image Capture

Once activated, the RGB camera captures multiple images or a short video of the moving object.

The captured data is immediately sent to the Jetson Nano for processing.

Step 4: AI-Based Elephant Detection

The Jetson Nano runs a trained deep learning model (such as YOLO) to identify the detected object.

The AI model classifies whether the object is:

Elephant
Human
Vehicle
Other Animal

If the detected object is not an elephant, the system returns to monitoring mode.

Step 5: Confidence Verification

To reduce false alarms, the AI verifies the detection confidence.

If confidence ≥ 80%, the object is confirmed as an elephant.
If confidence is below the threshold, the detection is ignored.

This improves system accuracy and reliability.

Step 6: Local Alert Activation

After confirming the presence of an elephant, the system immediately activates local warning devices such as:

High-decibel buzzer
Flashing LED light

These alerts help discourage elephants from approaching nearby villages or farmland.

Step 7: Long-Range Alert Transmission

The confirmed alert is transmitted through the LoRaWAN communication module.

Communication flow:

Detection Node
      │
      ▼
LoRaWAN Transmitter
      │
      ▼
LoRa Gateway
      │
      ▼
Forest Control Center

The alert contains:

Detection status
Date and time
Node ID
Detection confidence
GPS location (if available)

LoRaWAN provides:

Communication range of approximately 5–15 km
Low power consumption
Operation without a mobile network
Step 8: Event Logging

Each detection event is stored locally for future analysis.

The log includes:

Date
Time
Detection confidence
Image filename
Alert status

This information can be used to analyze elephant movement patterns and improve the system over time.

Step 9: Power Management

The entire system is powered using renewable energy.

Components:

100 W Solar Panel
LiFePO₄ Battery
Solar Charge Controller

Working:

Daytime: Solar panel powers the system and charges the battery.
Nighttime: The battery supplies uninterrupted power to the monitoring unit.

This allows the system to operate continuously in remote forest locations without external power.

Step 10: System Reset and Continuous Monitoring

After sending the alert and storing the event:

The camera stops recording.
The buzzer turns off after a predefined duration.
The system returns to low-power monitoring mode.

This cycle repeats continuously, ensuring 24×7 autonomous monitoring.
